b('Method: showing events are mutually exclusive / exhaustive.', 'Write the events as sets. Exclusive: check each pair has empty intersection. Exhaustive: check the union is S (counts add to n(S) if also disjoint)')

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 14.2 Axiomatic probability
d.sec('14.2-axioms')
b('Axioms of probability?', T('(i) P(E) ≥ 0') + ';  ' + T('(ii) P(S) = 1') + ';  ' + T('(iii) P(E ∪ F) = P(E) + P(F)') + ' for mutually exclusive E, F')
b('Prove P(φ) = 0 from the axioms.', 'E and φ are disjoint: P(E) = P(E ∪ φ) = P(E) + P(φ) → ' + N('P(φ) = 0'))
b('Probability on a finite sample space {ω₁, …, ωₙ}?', 'Assign P(ωᵢ) with 0 ≤ P(ωᵢ) ≤ 1 and ΣP(ωᵢ) = 1. Then P(A) = ' + T('Σ P(ωᵢ) over ωᵢ ∈ A'))
b('A coin: P(H) = ½ = P(T). Are P(H) = ¼, P(T) = ¾ also valid?', E('Yes') + '. Any p and 1 − p with 0 ≤ p ≤ 1 satisfy the axioms: infinitely many valid assignments. Equal probabilities are just one choice (the fair coin)')
b('Example 4: which assignments to six outcomes are valid: (a) six positive probabilities summing to 1, (b) 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, (c) with two negative values, (d) with 3/2, (e) 0.1, 0.2, …, 0.6?', '(a) ' + E('valid') + ' (b) ' + E('valid') + ' (c) ' + X('no: negative probabilities') + ' (d) ' + X('no: 3/2 > 1') + ' (e) ' + X('no: sum = 2.1'))
b('Validity test for a proposed assignment?', 'Check (1) every value in [0, 1] and (2) the values ' + T('sum to exactly 1') + '. Fail either and it is invalid')
b('Ex 14.2 Q1: which of the seven-outcome assignments are invalid: (c) 0.1, 0.2, …, 0.7; (d) −0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4, −0.2, 0.1, 0.3?', '(c) sums to ' + N('2.8') + ' → invalid. (d) has ' + N('negative values') + ' → invalid. (a) 0.1, 0.01, 0.05, 0.03, 0.01, 0.2, 0.6 sums to 1.0 → valid')
b('Equally likely outcomes: probability of an event?', r'\(P(E) = \dfrac{n(E)}{n(S)} = \dfrac{m}{n}\)' + ' (m favourable outcomes out of n equally likely outcomes)')
b('Why is P(each outcome) = 1/n for n equally likely outcomes?', 'The n probabilities are equal (p each) and sum to 1: np = 1 → ' + N('p = 1/n'))
b('Addition rule for any two events?', r'\(P(A \cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \cap B)\)' + '. If mutually exclusive, the last term is 0')
b('Why do we subtract P(A ∩ B)?', 'Outcomes in A ∩ B are counted ' + T('twice') + ' in P(A) + P(B). Example (3 coins): A = {HHT, HTH, THH}, B = {HTH, THH, HHH}: 3/8 + 3/8 = 6/8 but P(A ∪ B) = 4/8')
b('Proof of the addition rule (short)?', 'A ∪ B = A ∪ (B − A) (disjoint) and B = (A ∩ B) ∪ (B − A) (disjoint). Subtract: P(A ∪ B) − P(B) = P(A) − P(A ∩ B)')
b('Probability of “not A”?', T('P(A′) = 1 − P(A)') + ' since A and A′ are mutually exclusive and exhaustive: P(A) + P(A′) = 1')
b('Complement trick: P(at least one) = ?', T('1 − P(none)') + '. E.g. at least one tail in two tosses = 1 − P(HH) = 3/4')
b('Inclusion–exclusion for three events (Example 11)?', r'\(P(A\cup B\cup C) = \sum P(A) - \sum P(A\cap B) + P(A\cap B\cap C)\)')
sm.prob_venn(d)

